There is imported into Canada a very large number of these selfregistering scales, which are to be found in the butchers' shops and grocers' stores.
I am informed on very creditable authority that the United States people advertise that whoever purchases these scales may take 100 lbs. of beef, sell it in small quantities, and make five per cent. That is, they take a piece of beef and drop it on the self-registering scale. It will send the scale down to the lowest point, which is the point that registers what the consumer must pay for his beef, and the statement is made that a merchant can buy that self-registering scale, take 100 lbs. of beef or sugar, weigh it in small quantities, and he will make from three to five per cent. Our law does not permit these scales to be manufactured for the reason I have mentioned;

but if we do not permit their manufacture, we should not allow them to be imported, more particularly if they are sold under the advertisement to which I have drawn attention.

The hon. gentleman (Mr. Prefontaine) is showing most glaring ignorance of a most important Bill the carrying out of which is the duty of his department. What is the pilotage authority for instance, for the Quebec district ? The Quebec Harbour Commissioners ? The Pilotage Act, chapter 80, of the Revised Statutes of Canada, section 15, provides :
15. Subject to the provisions of this Act, or of any Act for the time being in force in its pilotage district, every pilotage authority shall have power, from time to time, by by-law confirmed by the Governor in Council, to do all or any of the following things within its district, that is to say :-
To determine the qualifications of pilots ; to make regulations respecting pilot boats ; to aid companies for the support of pilot boats ; to grant licenses and certificates ; to make regulations as to licenses and certificates ; to make regulations for the government of pilots; to make rules for punishing breaches of regulations ; to fix and alter pilotage dues ; compulsory retirement of aged pilots ; and of infirm and intemperate pilots ; decision of disputes about pilotage ; to establish and regulate pilot funds, except in Quebec and Montreal districts.
Now, that is very important, but it is not half so important as the other sections of this Act. When we come to the ^offices of pilots what do we see ? Section 73 of the Act says :
73. Every licensed pilot "who, either within or without the district for which he is licensed,
(a.) Commits any fraud or offence in respect to the revenues of customs or inland revenue or the laws relating thereto ;
(b.) Is in any way directly or indirectly concerned in any corrupt practices relating to ships, their tackle, cargoes, crews or passengers, or to persons in distress at sea or by shipwreck, or to their moneys, goods or chattels ;
(c.) Lends his license ;
(d.) Acts as pilot whilst suspended ;
(e.) Acts as pilot when in a state of intoxication ;
(f.) Employs or causes to be employed on behalf of any ship of which he has the charge, any steamboat, boat, anchor, cable, or other store, matter or thing, beyond wThat is necessary for the service of such ship, with the intent to enhance the expenses of pilotage for his own gain, or for the gain of any other person ;
(g.) Refuses or delays, when not prevented by illness or other reasonable cause, to take charge of any ship within the limits of his license, upon the signal for a pilot being made by such ship, or upon being required so to do by the master, owner, agent or consignee 1
thereof, or by any officer of the pilotage authority of the district of which such pilot is licensed or by any principal officer of customs, subject always in the case of a pilot for and below' the harbour of Quebec, to the laws relating to the corporation of pilots for and below- the harbour of Quebec ;
(h.) Upon being so signaled or required, attempts to make any special bargain for salvage ;
(i.) Unnecessarily cuts or slips, or causes to be cut or slipped, any cable belonging to any ship ;
(j.) Refuses, when requested by the master to conduct the ship on board of which he is into any port or place into which he is licensed to conduct the same, except on reasonable grounds of danger to the ship ; or-
(k.) Quits the ship which he has undertaken to pilot, before the service for which he has hired has been performed, without the consent of the master,

Shall, for each offence, in addition to any liability for damages, be liable to a penalty not exceeding two hundred dollars, and suspension or dismissal by the pilotage authority of the district for which he is licensed ; and every person who procures, abets or connives at the commission of such offence shall, for each offence, in addition to any liability or damages, be liable to a penalty not exceeding two hundred dollars, and if he is a licensed pilot, to suspension or dismissal by the pilotage authority of the district for which he is licensed.
Now my hon. friend would attribute to himself the power of trying these offences.
I admit my hon. friend is a lawyer, I admit he is probably as Capable of trying these offences as an ordinary police magistrate would be, or probably as competent as would be the pilotage authority, for instance, of the pilotage district of Quebec. But is my hon. friend, in the political position which he occupies now. going to take upon himself to try these offences and to impose a fine of $200 on any pilot who makes himself liable under the law ? I say that the hon. gentleman could not take that position, occupying the political position which he does, unless he is going to become a . udge to try these very important cases, and to impose fines upon those who render themselves guilty of the offences in question.

It provides that regulations can be made for the inspection of these boats. It has the effect of adding a new clause ' G ' to section 6, of the Steamship Inspection Act, 1898. In section 0 the following enumeration is made :
6. The Governor in Council may make rules and regulations for the following purposes, viz.:
(a.) for the testing of boilers and all matters connected with the construction and working thereof;

(b.) for the inspection of safety valves and boiler cocks and all matters connected with the construction and working thereof ;	.
(c.) for the inspection of hulls and equipment of steamboats ;
(d.) respecting boats and life-preservers, fire buckets, axes and lanterns and other life-saving appliances to be carried by steamboats or by other vessels mentioned in this Act ;
(e.) respecting the qualifications necessary to entitle a person to an engineer's certificate;
(f.) requiring steamboats to carry chemical or other fire extinguishers, and prescribing the number of such fire extinguishers to be carried by steamboats of different sizes and classes respectively.
Now, we add :
(g.) for the inspection of the machinery and equipment of steamboats propelled by gas, fluid, naphtha, electricity, or any other mechanical or chemical power, and in the case of such vessels for making such changes in forms A and B of the second schedule hereto as he deems advisable.
Under this amendment, special regulations will have to be prepared for the inspection of the class of boats which are enumerated here, but are not enumerated in the statute of 1898, or in the amendments which have since been made.
